(4544716, 650283, 58330, '<td valign="top">Thanks very much for all your comments about the cropping, which I found very helpful. Hope the pic below is a better effort.<br/> <br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=655427" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/55000-59999/58330/120/655427.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/55000-59999/58330/120/655427.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/55000-59999/58330/120/655427.jpg"/></a></td>', 'Thanks very much for all your comments about the cropping, which I found very helpful. Hope the pic below is a better effort. ', '2008-03-07 05:52:48.000000', NULL, 0),
(4544852, 650286, 7743, '<td valign="top">*** Critique Club ***<br/> <br/>First impressions: Focus seems to be in the right place, on the eyes<br/> <br/>Composition: I feeling that the image is a little too static.  This is probably because Kitters is a little too central.  By cropping a little on the right I think would help that.  The image could probably have been made more dynamic by using her front paw as a leading line into the image.  As it is the paw goes across the image.<br/> <br/>Exposure:  Shooting into bright light can be a real challenge.  I think fill flash or a reflector would have helped hear a great deal.  As it is Kitters face is a little dark.<br/> <br/>Impact:  I think impact is lost since Kitters face is dark.<br/> <br/>There is sparkle in the eye which is always good in a portrait.<br/> <br/>Have fun, keep shooting.<br/> <br/>Cheers!<br/> <br/>Colette</td>', '*** Critique Club *** First impressions: Focus seems to be in the right place, on the eyes Composition: I feeling that the image is a little too static.  This is probably because Kitters is a little too central.  By cropping a little on the right I think would help that.  The image could probably have been made more dynamic by using her front paw as a leading line into the image.  As it is the paw goes across the image. Exposure:  Shooting into bright light can be a real challenge.  I think fill flash or a reflector would have helped hear a great deal.  As it is Kitters face is a little dark. Impact:  I think impact is lost since Kitters face is dark. There is sparkle in the eye which is always good in a portrait. Have fun, keep shooting. Cheers! Colette', '2008-03-07 08:36:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(4545008, 649968, 91127, '<td valign="top">My 10 year old German Shepherd JoJo died on Feb. 19th. When I saw this, I believe it''s the first time I have smiled since that morning.  Although this photograph is aesthetically beautiful and it deserves high scores for that, for me, it is a 10 for many other reasons.</td>', 'My 10 year old German Shepherd JoJo died on Feb. 19th. When I saw this, I believe it''s the first time I have smiled since that morning.  Although this photograph is aesthetically beautiful and it deserves high scores for that, for me, it is a 10 for many other reasons.', '2008-03-07 09:33:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(4545075, 650557, 30049, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/><u>First Impression</u> <br/> <br/>Cool patterns.  Interesting animal.  I want more.<br/> <br/><u>Looking Deeper</u> <br/> <br/>Beautiful animal, for sure!  I think that''s why I was a little disappointed/unsatisfied when I first saw this photo during the challenge.  Unsatisfied because I wanted more detail and to encounter the richness of this wonderful cat in a more intimate way.  This portrait feels a little static and distant in other words.<br/> <br/>As someone (you) who knows this cat the photo is fine as you can relate to the cat''s personality and traits.<br/> <br/>What would change the way I see it?  The head, face, and eyes...engage me, show me some soul.  The cat now is looking down and away.  Very impersonal.  The fur, the whiskers, the texture and detail...bring me in closer.  A "head &amp; shoulder" type portrait would still show the character (unique fur patterns) but let the viewer feel like they''re in the moment.<br/> <br/>Technically, the photo is "ok".  A little deeper aperture would have brought the entire face and distant eye into sharper focus.  Lighting and exposure is fine also.<br/> <br/>It''s not the technicals that held this back IMO.  It''s the documentary, static feel of the image overall.<br/> <br/>Anyway, that''s my two cents.  :-)<br/> <br/>Questions?  Please feel free to send me a PM.  Thanks!</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club First Impression  Cool patterns.  Interesting animal.  I want more. Looking Deeper  Beautiful animal, for sure!  I think that''s why I was a little disappointed/unsatisfied when I first saw this photo during the challenge.  Unsatisfied because I wanted more detail and to encounter the richness of this wonderful cat in a more intimate way.  This portrait feels a little static and distant in other words. As someone (you) who knows this cat the photo is fine as you can relate to the cat''s personality and traits. What would change the way I see it?  The head, face, and eyes...engage me, show me some soul.  The cat now is looking down and away.  Very impersonal.  The fur, the whiskers, the texture and detail...bring me in closer.  A "head & shoulder" type portrait would still show the character (unique fur patterns) but let the viewer feel like they''re in the moment. Technically, the photo is "ok".  A little deeper aperture would have brought the entire face and distant eye into sharper focus.  Lighting and exposure is fine also. It''s not the technicals that held this back IMO.  It''s the documentary, static feel of the image overall. Anyway, that''s my two cents.  :-) Questions?  Please feel free to send me a PM.  Thanks!', '2008-03-07 09:45:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(4545285, 650660, 81530, '<td valign="top">This photograph is a lot better than the score it received. It is nice to see a different approach. I wish people were a little more open minded. The only constructive criticism I have is that the photograph appears bottom heavy since there isn''t much negative space below the dogs head compared to the negative space up top.<br/></td>', 'This photograph is a lot better than the score it received. It is nice to see a different approach. I wish people were a little more open minded. The only constructive criticism I have is that the photograph appears bottom heavy since there isn''t much negative space below the dogs head compared to the negative space up top.', '2008-03-07 10:56:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(4545498, 650414, 80096, '<td valign="top">I think a tad of levels on this would have improved it significantly. Otherwise an excellent portrait, beautiful feline.</td>', 'I think a tad of levels on this would have improved it significantly. Otherwise an excellent portrait, beautiful feline.', '2008-03-07 12:06:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(4545649, 650539, 74559, '<td valign="top">Ah, I have a couple grumpy cats too so I can relate! Her expression is great!</td>', 'Ah, I have a couple grumpy cats too so I can relate! Her expression is great!', '2008-03-07 13:18:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(4546157, 650259, 811, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUE<br/>by karmat<br/> <br/>Wow, nice shot.  I am truly surprised this didn''t score higher.<br/> <br/>Compositionally, you have filled the frame nicely and it feels well balanced.  Technically, it is excellently (is that a word) focused and the details on the "skin" and in the eye is tremendous.  Perhaps the slight blur on the end of the nose put some people off, but to me, it just helps to reinforce how great the focus is on the eye.<br/> <br/>The *only* thing that disconcerts me (other than the fact that I thought it was a snake at first) is the "mismatch" of colors in the background.  The purplish hues contrast nicely with the yellow.  The "flesh tones'' for lack of a better word, just don''t "match" to me for some reason.<br/> <br/>Still, though, very nice shot!<br/> <br/>karma</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUEby karmat Wow, nice shot.  I am truly surprised this didn''t score higher. Compositionally, you have filled the frame nicely and it feels well balanced.  Technically, it is excellently (is that a word) focused and the details on the "skin" and in the eye is tremendous.  Perhaps the slight blur on the end of the nose put some people off, but to me, it just helps to reinforce how great the focus is on the eye. The *only* thing that disconcerts me (other than the fact that I thought it was a snake at first) is the "mismatch" of colors in the background.  The purplish hues contrast nicely with the yellow.  The "flesh tones'' for lack of a better word, just don''t "match" to me for some reason. Still, though, very nice shot! karma', '2008-03-07 16:14:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(4546179, 649970, 811, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUE<br/>by karmat<br/> <br/>Cool shot.<br/> <br/>Compositionally, I like the location of the fish in the frame and how his "shape and direction" kinda mirrors the stuff in the background as far as direction goes.  Technically, the colors and focus in this are great.  Enough color without being oversaturated, and they compliment nicely.<br/> <br/>I seriously don''t know what could improve it.  Maybe some of the voters were put off looking at a fish at this angle?  It looks unnatural, or something?  Not a lot of help, I know, but I seriously don''t know what to do differently to make this any better.<br/> <br/>karmat</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUEby karmat Cool shot. Compositionally, I like the location of the fish in the frame and how his "shape and direction" kinda mirrors the stuff in the background as far as direction goes.  Technically, the colors and focus in this are great.  Enough color without being oversaturated, and they compliment nicely. I seriously don''t know what could improve it.  Maybe some of the voters were put off looking at a fish at this angle?  It looks unnatural, or something?  Not a lot of help, I know, but I seriously don''t know what to do differently to make this any better. karmat', '2008-03-07 16:31:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(4546251, 650225, 811, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUE<br/>by karmat<br/> <br/>5.3????  Seriously underrated.<br/> <br/>The composition of this is superb.  The dog is located in a strong place in the frame, and there is a lot of "space" for it to travel into.  The light forms some nice lines leading the eyes to the dog.  Perhaps a lower perspective of the dog (as one commenter suggested) might help, but this seems very natural feeling.<br/> <br/>Technically, the focus is great.  On my monitor, I can see details on the shadowed side, but I''m wondering if some darker monitors obscured this so that it looks harsher than it really is.<br/> <br/>Sorry that I''m not a lot of help, but I think the shot is very strong as it is.  <br/> <br/>Karma</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUEby karmat 5.3????  Seriously underrated. The composition of this is superb.  The dog is located in a strong place in the frame, and there is a lot of "space" for it to travel into.  The light forms some nice lines leading the eyes to the dog.  Perhaps a lower perspective of the dog (as one commenter suggested) might help, but this seems very natural feeling. Technically, the focus is great.  On my monitor, I can see details on the shadowed side, but I''m wondering if some darker monitors obscured this so that it looks harsher than it really is. Sorry that I''m not a lot of help, but I think the shot is very strong as it is.   Karma', '2008-03-07 16:55:35.000000', NULL, 0),
